{
 "schema": 1,
 "doctrine": [
  "A menu of starting points, never a fence: any role or level may be bent, combined or replaced by an invented audience ('invented: <name>' plus a reason).",
  "The audience tailors the DESIGN (depth, entry point, register, analogy sources, pace). It is never a person the deliverable greets, welcomes or role-plays.",
  "The audience is inferred from the brief and its context. It is never defaulted to a child or to 'age 5'; when the signals are too weak, the Phase-0 questions ask.",
  "'Explain like I'm five' (and its four-letter abbreviation) is read as 'keep it simple', not as a five-year-old audience.",
  "Purpose before mechanism: every audience hears what a thing is for before how it works.",
  "Business audiences hear the impact and the decision first ('this means we should ...').",
  "When the audience is expert in the subject's field, keep that field's terms; simplify only what lies outside their specialty.",
  "Getting the core idea across mostly accurately beats a fully accurate version that loses the reader, but that trade is always flagged and must never teach something false."
 ],
 "levels": [
  {
   "id": "child",
   "order": 0,
   "label": "child (about 6-12)",
   "register_shift": "short, warm, concrete; one idea per sentence; never baby talk",
   "sentence_words_max": 12,
   "jargon": "none; an essential word is defined the moment it appears",
   "depth": "the one core idea and what it does",
   "entry_point": "a thing they can see, touch or play with",
   "analogy_sources": [
    "games",
    "animals",
    "school",
    "toys",
    "playground",
    "cartoons",
    "sports"
   ],
   "video_pace": "slow; one picture per idea"
  },
  {
   "id": "teen",
